1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,042
What if there are more unknowns than equations?

2
00:00:09,042 --> 00:00:12,972
Let's take the smallest case, with one equation and two unknowns.

3
00:00:12,972 --> 00:00:17,991
Every point on this line fits the data perfectly, so the loss is zero all along it,

4
00:00:17,991 --> 00:00:22,033
and the question is which of them gradient descent will pick.

5
00:00:22,033 --> 00:00:24,979
Here's the row of X, which points along one, two.

6
00:00:24,979 --> 00:00:30,872
The null direction runs along the line instead, and sliding that way changes no prediction at all.

7
00:00:30,872 --> 00:00:35,622
These two directions are perpendicular, and together they fill the whole plane,

8
00:00:35,622 --> 00:00:40,366
which is the fundamental theorem of linear algebra.

9
00:00:40,366 --> 00:00:42,229
Now look at the gradient step.

10
00:00:42,229 --> 00:00:46,762
It's X transpose times something, so every update lives in the row space.

11
00:00:46,762 --> 00:00:52,400
That means the null-space part of w never moves, and only the row-space part changes.

12
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,320
So let's start at zero and watch.

13
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:59,559
We walk straight along the row direction, and we stop the moment we hit the solution line.

14
00:00:59,559 --> 00:01:07,033
That stopping point is special, since it's the minimum-norm solution, and in this problem it's the point one, two.

15
00:01:07,033 --> 00:01:08,785
Why is that one the smallest?

16
00:01:08,785 --> 00:01:14,644
Every other solution is this one with a perpendicular null piece added, so it can only be longer.

17
00:01:14,644 --> 00:01:23,466
Take the solution at five, zero, where the squared length is twenty-five, which is five plus twenty.

18
00:01:23,466 --> 00:01:26,839
Now start somewhere else, at the point three, minus two.

19
00:01:26,839 --> 00:01:32,200
We travel in the same direction as before, but this time we land on a different solution.

20
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:39,200
The null part of the start just rode along, frozen, so we end up at the minimum-norm solution plus that piece.

21
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:46,070
So gradient descent lands on the solution nearest its start, and starting at zero is what gives the minimum-norm one.

22
00:01:46,070 --> 00:01:53,433
The loss can't tell these solutions apart, so here the starting point is what makes the choice.

23
00:01:53,433 --> 00:01:56,286
With more dimensions the story is the same.

24
00:01:56,286 --> 00:02:03,054
Here are two equations and three unknowns, and in the SVD basis each coordinate of w moves on its own,

25
00:02:03,054 --> 00:02:07,300
so let's just watch them.

26
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:12,738
Two of the coordinates settle, keeping forty percent and eighty percent of their error per step,

27
00:02:12,738 --> 00:02:14,551
while the null one never budges.

28
00:02:14,551 --> 00:02:22,833
Real networks have far more parameters than data, so the optimizer and its starting point are what pick the answer.

